Development Behaviour
Do not explain what you are about to do.
Do not provide implementation plans before coding.
Do not summarise the specification back to the user.
Do not generate TODO lists.
Do not create placeholder implementations.
Do not defer functionality described in this specification.
Do not ask whether additional features should also be implemented.
Implement the complete specification in this build.
When finished, provide only a concise summary of the changes made and any genuine technical limitations encountered.

Code Quality Rules
Improve existing code rather than creating alternative implementations.
If an existing component must change, refactor it instead of creating a new version.
Do not leave obsolete components, hooks, services or database entities in the project after replacement.
Reuse existing code whenever practical.
Prefer extending existing functionality over introducing parallel systems.

Review the existing application before making any changes. 

Once you understand the current implementation, begin implementing the requested functionality immediately. 

Do not stop to explain your plan. 

Do not ask for confirmation before implementing. 

Do not ask for permission to proceed. 

Do not describe the implementation you intend to perform before performing it. 

Design Rules
For all designs I ask you to make, have them be beautiful, not cookie cutter. Make webpages that are fully featured and worthy for production.

Other Rules

By default, this template supports JSX syntax with Tailwind CSS classes, React hooks, and Lucide React for icons. Do not install other packages for UI themes, icons, etc unless absolutely necessary or I request them.

Use icons from lucide-react for logos.

Import project modules with the `@/` path alias, which maps to `src/` (e.g. `@/components/Foo` == `src/components/Foo`), instead of deep relative paths like `../../components/Foo`.